---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import NTLink from '@drift/ui/NTLink';
---


<Base title="About Us">
  <div class="about-content">
    <h1 class:list={[
      'text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold',
      'mb-7',
    ]}>
      About Us
    </h1>

    <div class="prose">

      <section id="intro">
        <p>
          Drift is a programming language written in Kotlin and powered by the Java Virtual Machine.
          It is designed to be expressive, familiar, and practical, drawing on the best ideas from
          Java, Kotlin, C#, Swift, and TypeScript while standing on its own terms.
        </p>

        <p>
          The language began as an interpreted prototype in its 2025.0 release, a sandbox to explore
          syntax and semantics. With Gotthard, the first major evolution of Drift, that prototype gives
          way to a proper JVM-based compiler. Drift sources are now compiled to JVM bytecode through a
          full pipeline: lexing, parsing, type checking, and bytecode emission via ASM, enabling real
          performance and interoperability with the broader Java ecosystem.
        </p>

        <p>
          Gotthard also ships Homemade, the Drift standard library. It covers primary types, collections,
          I/O, and the core utilities every Drift program needs. More specialized functionality is
          distributed as separate packages called kits, keeping the language core focused and the
          ecosystem composable.
        </p>

        <p>
          Drift is developed openly on GitHub. It is a language for people who want to write code that
          is clear to read, straightforward to reason about, and ready to grow.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="birth">
        <h2>
          Born To Be Experimental And Educational
        </h2>

        <p>
          Drift started as a student side project — a way to learn how programming languages actually
          work by building one from scratch. The 2025.0 prototype was never intended for production;
          it was a space to experiment with syntax ideas, understand interpreters, and figure out what
          kind of language Drift could become. That origin is not something to hide. It shaped the
          project's values: curiosity over convention, learning by doing, and no fear of starting over
          when a better approach is found.
        </p>

        <p>
          That spirit carries forward into Gotthard and beyond. Drift is still a place to explore
          language design in the open, and it is built with the hope that others — students, hobbyists,
          curious developers — will find it useful as a reference for how a JVM language is constructed,
          from the first token to bytecode output.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>
            Since Gotthard, Drift has fixed its syntax and conventions. It tends to be more stable,
            version after version.
          </strong>
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="os">
        <h2>
          An Open-Source Project
        </h2>

        <p>
          Drift is and will always be free and open-source. The compiler, the standard library, and
          all official tooling live in the open on GitHub, where anyone can read the source, report
          issues, and contribute. There is no commercial tier, no proprietary runtime, and no part of
          the language hidden behind a paywall. The project is built this way by choice: open
          development produces better software, and a language that belongs to its community is one
          that can be trusted.
        </p>
      </section>

      <hr />

      <section class="flex flex-col gap-y-1" id="links">
        <NTLink href="https://github.com/drift-language">Our GitHub Organization</NTLink>
        <NTLink href="https://github.com/drift-language/drift">Star Our Main Repository!</NTLink>
        <NTLink href="https://github.com/orgs/drift-language/projects/1">Our Project Board</NTLink>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>